      {
        "text": "## Ruha Benjamin\n\n### Technology & Social Justice\n\n*Image attribution: [Ruha Benjamin at Databites 2019](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruha_Benjamin,_Databite_124,_2019_(cropped).jpg) by Data & Society Research Institute, via Wikimedia Commons. CC BY 3.0.*\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/6340be1e394dadcd81f4e6f65107b549-benjamin2.jpg",
        "key": "aif-ai-powered-threats-defenses-lesson2-ddos-panels-37f6d857-ab3b-4e1e-b18d-ce3bfaa38a45",
        "fadeInOverPrevious": false,
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      },
      {
        "text": "### What She Contributed\n\nBenjamin is a scholar of science, technology, and society whose work examines how technologies — including AI — can appear neutral while reinforcing existing inequalities. Her book *Race After Technology* introduced the concept of the “New Jim Code” to describe how discriminatory practices can be embedded in digital tools and algorithms.<sup>1</sup>\n\n*Image attribution: AI-generated illustration created with ChatGPT (OpenAI), 2026.*\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/706826f032823f9a4a857be1cf329f63-technology.jpg",
        "key": "aif-ai-powered-threats-defenses-lesson2-ddos-panels-1eab77e5-f2ef-4fdf-b059-19dd3b604abd",
        "layout": "text-bottom-right"
      },
      {
        "text": "### Connection to AI History\n\nBenjamin’s contributions belong to the critical study of AI’s social impact. Her work connects AI bias to broader histories of race, inequality, and institutional design — pushing researchers to ask not just whether a system is accurate, but who it serves and who it may harm.<sup>2</sup>\n\n*Image attribution: AI-generated illustration created with ChatGPT (OpenAI), 2026.*\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/555fa7ef33e65e6c3c3356089ead5f71-technology2.jpg",
        "key": "aif-ai-powered-threats-defenses-lesson2-ddos-panels-0c7ff7a4-4735-4c26-84d0-b8872e1b2b50",
        "layout": "text-bottom-center"
      },
      {
        "text": "### Why It Matters\n\nBy bringing social science and humanities perspectives to AI, Benjamin helps explain why technical accuracy alone is not enough to make a system fair. Her scholarship pushes researchers, designers, and policymakers to examine whose interests AI systems are built to serve.<sup>1</sup>\n\n*Image attribution: AI-generated illustration created with ChatGPT (OpenAI), 2026.*\n\n**Sources**\n\n1. [MacArthur Foundation — Ruha Benjamin](https://www.macfound.org/fellows/class-of-2024/ruha-benjamin)\n2. [Institute for Advanced Study — Race After Technology](https://www.ias.edu/ideas/race-after-technology)\n",
        "imageUrl": "https://images.code.org/434b4d886b8064cb6d91e2b687c7361a-technology3.jpg",
        "key": "aif-ai-powered-threats-defenses-lesson2-ddos-panels-87ad2c1f-b495-4264-a7c0-51cce6ca9fff",
        "layout": "text-top-left"
      }
    ],
    "name_suffix": "_year1-2026",
    "display_name": "Ruha Benjamin",
    "bubble_choice_description": "Choose Ruha Benjamin to explore how her work reveals ways AI can reinforce inequality and challenges people to build technology that serves society more fairly.",
